    "sample_id": "test:3409",
    "setup_id": "05601a00c6bc4954635863826740b7b61d18798ad2f99e32a9fef415a652990f",
    "text": "To determine the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\), we need to analyze the units digits of the powers of 3. Let's start by examining the units digits of the first few powers of 3:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^1 & = 3 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^2 & = 9 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^3 & = 27 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^4 & = 81 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n3^5 & = 243 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^6 & = 729 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^7 & = 2187 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^8 & = 6561 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe observe that the units digits repeat every 4 terms: 3, 9, 7, 1. Therefore, the units digits of \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots\\) form a repeating sequence of 3, 7, 3, 1.\n\nNext, we need to count the number of terms in the sequence \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots, 3^{2009}\\). This is an arithmetic sequence with the first term \\(a = 1\\) and common difference \\(d = 2\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence is given by:\n\n\\[\na_n = a + (n-1)d = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 = 2n - 1\n\\]\n\nWe set \\(a_n = 2n - 1\\) equal to the last term \\(3^{2009}\\):\n\n\\[\n2n - 1 = 2009 \\implies 2n = 2010 \\implies n = 1005\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 1005 terms in the sequence. Since the units digits repeat every 4 terms, we need to find the units digit of the sum of 1005 terms. We do this by finding the units digit of the sum of one complete cycle (4 terms) and then multiplying by the number of complete cycles, followed by adding the units digit of the remaining terms.\n\nThe units digits of one complete cycle (4 terms) are 3, 7, 3, 1. The sum of these units digits is:\n\n\\[\n3 + 7 + 3 + 1 = 14 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 4)}\n\\]\n\nSince 1005 terms include 251 complete cycles (1004 terms) and 1 additional term (the 1005th term, which is the first term of the next cycle):\n\n\\[\n251 \\text{ complete cycles} \\times 4 \\text{ terms per cycle} = 1004 \\text{ terms}\n\\]\n\nThe units digit of the sum of 1004 terms is:\n\n\\[\n1004 \\times 4 = 4016 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 6)}\n\\]\n\nNow, we add the units digit of the 1005th term, which is 3:\n\n\\[\n6 + 3 = 9\n\\]\n\nThus, the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
